<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léon M'ba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1902–1967</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Léon Mba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léon M'ba was a Gabonese politician who led Gabon to independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 17 August 1960 and became its first president. He kept close economic and military ties with France and built a one-party state. In 1964 a military coup briefly removed him, but French troops restored him to power. He died in office in 1967 and was succeeded by Omar Bongo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leon-mba.jpg" alt="Léon M'ba" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9on_M%27ba_1964.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
